nt we were  better able to provide  the same standard of care that we would  normally do  so it's probably a combination of things  but there is no doubt  that if you become sick with covid if  you come into hospital and certainly if  you are  admitted to an intensive care unit you  have a much better chance of surviving  now than you did  at the beginning of the year yeah  genuinely uh sort of good news  reassuring  um there has been some speculation that  it could be because the virus is  mutating to become weaker or because  people are getting a  lower viral load because of social  distancing do you think either those  things could be having an impact as well  i've not heard anything that would  suggest that the  nature of the virus has changed and  i i'm sure that you know as as sir  jeremy um  just mentioned that that many many  people  are actually listening to the guidance  we're not  um socializing as much and that will  have had an impact in terms of community  transmission  but it is still a very deadly virus so  although the majority of people  still who who become infected will have  a very very minor  illness or may not even know that  they're ill at all  for those people that require hospital  admission  for those that come to intensive care  it's still a very  severe disease and we certainly see in  critical  care that patients are extremely sick  and we've seen the images on  on on tv from inside liverpool hospital  and that you know that the staff are  working really hard their patients are  really sick and so it's it is important  that we  appreciate that the disease is still  there it's still  severe and if you end up in critical  care with covert pneumonia  you're almost twice as likely to die  than somebody who's admitted with a  pneumonia not due to covid  so it's still something to be worried  about really important um  distinctions there you were talking  there about the situation in some  hospitals in the areas that have been  worse  hit more recently because  hospitalizations really are starting to  tick up again so  what is the situation in intensive care  and  how concerned are you about nhs capacity  because we have heard of some  hospitals in nottingham liverpool  already beginning to cancel non-urgent  appointments  i think it's very different around the  country  you know we've seen the hot spots on the  the maps that we see on the  media and certainly the north west and  northeast yorkshire and humber  um parts of the midlands are are  affected and liverpool in particular  as we're all aware of um and in those  critical care units  what everyone is trying to do is unlike  the first wave where almost all  normal nhs activity was stopped apart  from urgent and emergency care  we don't want to see the unintended  consequences of that during this  next wave so we're trying to make sure  that we have  safe pathways for patients with covid  and those  without covid so that we can continue to  treat  everybody that needs to come to hospital  and so what that means is in intensive  care units at the moment  not all of those patients will be sick  with covid because we are continuing to  do  as much normal activity as we can  but unfortunately if covid continues to  rise in terms of community transmission  that is  by definition going to translate into  more patients in hospital  and more patients and critical care and  therefore the only way we can do that is  by stopping  non-urgent care and so the consequences  again this time round isn't going to be  on covered patients getting access to  health care it's going to be for those  patients that  don't have covid so it may well have an  impact on people who have chronic ill  health who need to have surveillance  it may be pregnant women who need  antenatal care  who may be scared to come forward  because they don't want to either risk  becoming infected or they don't want to  overburden the nhs and that is the one  thing that we we want to avoid this time  so if we can keep community transmission  down  it means that we can treat everybody  that needs health care and that is the  great desire for everyone working in the  nhs at the moment
 

